    {
      "id": "ROB-009",
      "category": "appendix_mismatch",
      "finding_summary": "Appendix A.3.2 says words fitting several types get the first type in Table 9, but its own example (bysykkelstativ as infrastructure) and Figure 2 (bysykkelstativ and syklende coded as infrastructure) contradict that rule given Table 9's order, so the type shares in Table 8 are not reproducible from the stated rule.",
      "issue_type": "manuscript_issue",
      "severity": "medium",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "location": {
        "page": 18,
        "page_label": "18",
        "section": "A.3.2 Classification of cycling mentions",
        "text_quote": "given the first type in the table.",
        "precision": "exact"
      },
      "claim_text": "If a word fits more than one type, it is given the first type in the table. For example, words containing 'bysykkel', such as 'bysykkelstativ', are counted as infrastructure.",
      "assessment": "no",
      "cannot_verify_reason": null,
      "evidence_summary": "Table 9 lists Parking & storage (containing 'stativ') and Proximity (containing 'sykle') before Infrastructure. 'bysykkelstativ' contains both 'bysykkel' and 'stativ', and 'syklende' (listed under Infrastructure) contains 'sykle' (listed under Proximity). A literal first-match rule would classify them as parking and proximity. The page-16 image shows both coloured as Infrastructure in Figure 2. The text concedes the rule is coarse and some corner cases are misclassified, but does not give the actual precedence used. The size of the effect on Table 8 shares is not determinable from the manuscript. Infrastructure remains the largest category in any case since moving words out of Infrastructure would reduce it, but the Proximity/Parking ordering (10.8% vs 10.7%) is sensitive. Table 9 was read from page text because the structured table extraction is unreliable.",
